Revision history for Test::Consumer::Contract

0.03
	- The scanner keeps an inventory of its own rules: one name per rule, with the
	  sub the rule is exercised through, returned by scanner_capabilities().  It is
	  in the module because it has to move with it, and t/contract.t fails when a
	  rule in it has no mutation subtest, when a mutation subtest names a rule that
	  is not in it, and when a row names a sub the module does not define.  A rule
	  nobody has shown catching anything is a rule that may be doing nothing, so a
	  new behaviour of the scanner now has to arrive with the failure it catches.
	- Every mutation subtest goes through one helper, which records the rule it
	  proved, so the guard sees every mutation there is rather than the ones that
	  remembered to register themselves.
	- A value object read as a class of its own distribution: new options
	  list_methods, for a call that hands back a list of results, and
	  own_consumers, for the distribution that makes the value object being one of
	  its readers.  The loop variable of a loop over such a list - written over the
	  call, over a list assigned from it, or as the topic of a map or grep block
	  over it - is a result, and its reads are checked like any other consumer's.
	- A documented accessor is read with its parentheses or without them, and a
	  helper that takes the result after its invocant is followed: reading
	  $entry->memo, and reading the entry in the $self->_line($entry) it is passed
	  to, used to find nothing at all.
	- A producing call may be named with its class - Widget::Maker->fetch, or
	  Widget::Maker::fetch - so that a constructor shared with every other object
	  in the file binds only its own results.
	- Fixed: POD with no =cut (common after __END__) and anything after __END__
	  or __DATA__ were read as code, so examples in them became reads.
	- Fixed: a one-line sub ran on to the next sub's closing brace, and reads in
	  that sub were reported under the wrong name.
	- Fixed: sets_key() took '=~' and '==' for assignments, and accepted
	  mismatched quotes; a comparison with a producing call bound the variable
	  compared.
	- Fixed: a value object that is in @INC but does not compile, or whose
	  constructor dies, now fails check 2 rather than being skipped or ending the
	  test file.  An absent value object is still skipped.
	- Fixed: an unreadable producer or consumer now says why, with the OS error.
	- Fixed: an optional option given as undef was stored as undef, and run()
	  then died; it now takes its default.
	- POD rewritten in plainer English, with COMMON PITFALLS, ENCODING, a
	  FORMAL SPECIFICATION section and a STATE DIAGRAM.
	- Fixed: the accessor check used two different ideas of "documented" for its
	  two directions; both now use documented_accessors().
	- new() validates the declaration (Params::Validate::Strict): wrong types,
	  malformed 'ignored' rules and consumer entries, a value object class that is
	  not a module name, and an explicit dist_root or workspace that is not a
	  directory are errors.  It accepts one hashref as well as a list.
	- Every user-facing string goes through i18n(), with a 'messages' option and
	  %config{messages} for rewording, sprintf placeholders and plural/context
	  forms.  The English text is unchanged.
	- reads_of_results() and documented_accessors() return an arrayref in scalar
	  context (a list in list context, as before).
	- Private subs are enforced with Sub::Private, and the checks are protected
	  with Sub::Protected so a subclass can override them.  Perl 5.14 is now the
	  minimum (Sub::Private needs it).
	- include no longer adds duplicate @INC entries when several contracts are
	  built.  xt/ was listed as a sibling directory to scan but always pruned; it
	  is no longer listed.

	- methods is required, and has no default.  It used to default to geocode,
	  reverse_geocode and geocode_many, which made the checker a geocoder checker:
	  a contract for anything else was silently checking nothing, because the names
	  it guessed were not in the source it was reading.  The calls that hand a
	  result back are the caller's to name - [qw(fetch scan)] works as well as
	  [qw(geocode)] - and new() now says so rather than guessing.  batch_methods
	  has no default either, so a contract with no batches is the plain case.
	- An empty method list finds nothing.  It used to compile to an alternation
	  that matches at every position, which made every '(' in the source a
	  producing call and every key after one a read of a result; a contract that
	  named no methods would have passed while reporting reads nobody made.
	- t/contract.t runs the three checks over a second value object with no
	  geocoding anywhere in it - a warehouse item fetched from a store, declared
	  with methods [qw(fetch scan)] - so a geocoder assumption creeping back into
	  the checks fails there rather than in a workspace that cannot be checked.
	- t/scanner.t pins the same two things directly: fetch() hands a result under
	  a contract that names it, geocode() does not under a contract that does not,
	  and a contract with no producing calls finds no reads at all.

0.02	Sat Oct 17 08:51:01 AM EDT 2026
	- Discovery now sees extensionless programs.  A file with no extension and a
	  shebang is a consumer like any other, whether it lives in bin/ or at the
	  distribution root: a script can hold the same geocoding loop as the module
	  beside it, and the reader/producer contract used to walk past both
	  HTML-Genealogy-Map/ged2map and the root of a distribution entirely.
	- geocode_many() is a producing method.  A variable bound from a batch is not
	  a result - its counts are not fields of one - while a value taken out of the
	  batch is, so the reads off that value are checked.  Database::BI's place
	  column resolution was invisible until this: it reads the position out of a
	  batch and nothing else.
	- A value taken out of a batch is followed wherever it is put, not only into
	  a variable of its own: into a hash, where a read through an element is a
	  read of a result, and back out of a sub through a return, whose callers are
	  sources of results in turn.  A consumer that keeps a batch's values in a
	  hash of its own, or hands one back out of a helper, was read as a consumer
	  of nothing at all; Database::BI happens to bind into a variable first, so
	  this is the shape the fixtures pin rather than one the workspace holds.
	  reads_of_results() also reports in file order now, so a report reads
	  top-down rather than in the order the several passes happened to find.
	- t/contract.t proves each capability by mutation.  With the shebang rule
	  removed an unregistered program is invisible; with batch support removed
	  neither geocode_many() failure is found; with the batch treated as a
	  result its own count is reported as a read ('lookups'); and with the hash
	  and return following removed the two reads through them are missed.  Each
	  mutation runs the same fixture in a subprocess with the checker patched, so
	  the new tests are shown to fail because of the capability and not by
	  accident.
	- workspace_present() answers before a check has run.  It read the cache of
	  missing siblings directly instead of filling it, and died on an undefined
	  array reference the first time it was asked.

0.01	Fri Oct 16 08:52:09 PM EDT 2026
	- First version, lifted from the reader/producer contract that lived inside
	  Geo-Coder-Free/t/consumer-contract.t, so any distribution in the workspace
	  can declare its own producer keys and registered consumers and get the same
	  three checks.
